---
registryId: nginx
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is NGINX?

NGINX is an event-driven web server and traffic proxy. It can accept a client
connection, terminate TLS, select a virtual server and location, enforce request
limits, serve a local or cached response, or proxy the request to an upstream
application.

A **reverse proxy** gives a system one controlled boundary in front of its services.
That boundary matters because routing, client identity, timeouts, retries, buffering,
caching, and overload policy all change what the application receives and what the
caller observes.

NGINX does not make an unhealthy application reliable by itself. A timeout can leave an
upstream result unknown, a retry can repeat a mutation, a cache can serve the wrong
tenant, and a broad location rule can route traffic somewhere unintended. The safe
design starts with the end-to-end request contract, then configures NGINX to preserve
it.
{% /section-card %}

{% callout variant="info" %}
New to request routing? Review [communication patterns](/fundamentals/communication-patterns)
and [load balancing](/fundamentals/load-balancing) before tuning proxy failure behavior.
{% /callout %}

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Web server" eyebrow="Respond locally" icon="server" tone="blue" %}
Serve static files, redirects, errors, health endpoints, or generated module responses
without opening an upstream connection.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Reverse proxy" eyebrow="Forward intentionally" icon="route" tone="cyan" %}
Normalize the request boundary, select an upstream, apply deadlines and buffering, and
return a controlled response.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Load balancer" eyebrow="Distribute work" icon="network" tone="violet" %}
Choose among upstream servers with round robin, least connections, hashing, weights,
and passive or configured health behavior.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="HTTP cache" eyebrow="Avoid source work" icon="database" tone="green" %}
Store reusable responses under an explicit identity and freshness policy, then serve a
hit or permitted stale response without contacting the origin.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Trace one proxied request through the edge

NGINX first chooses a listening socket and virtual server, then evaluates the request
against location rules. A selected location may reject, redirect, serve, cache, or
proxy. The upstream response then crosses buffering, header, cache, and error policy
before reaching the client.

{% system-flow title="One reverse-proxy request" caption="Every arrow is a contract boundary: client identity, routing, cache identity, upstream attempt, and returned result must remain observable." %}
{% system-node title="Client connection" eyebrow="Protocol boundary" icon="globe" tone="blue" %}
Negotiate TLS and HTTP, bound headers and body size, and preserve a request ID and
deadline that can be correlated across the system.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Server and location" eyebrow="Routing boundary" icon="route" tone="violet" %}
Match host, port, path, method, and explicit policy. Confirm exact and prefix locations
do not shadow sensitive handlers.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Cache or upstream" eyebrow="Work boundary" icon="database" tone="cyan" %}
Serve an eligible cache entry or choose an upstream and apply connect, send, read,
retry, buffering, and request-body behavior.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Client response" eyebrow="Outcome boundary" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Return the status, headers, and body while recording edge time, upstream time, cache
status, selected upstream, and final attempt result.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Make routing behavior explicit

- Define one owner for each host and path; test normal, trailing-slash, encoded,
  traversal-like, and unexpected-method requests.
- Keep internal administration and status locations inaccessible from untrusted
  networks.
- Pass a deliberate `Host` value. The default proxy behavior is not necessarily the
  original client host.
- Use generated configuration tests for every route and policy boundary before reload.
- Avoid variable-heavy `proxy_pass` rules until DNS resolution, URI rewriting, cache
  keys, and observability have been tested together.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Understand the master, workers, and connection slots

The master process reads configuration, binds sockets, responds to control signals,
and manages worker generations. Workers run event loops and handle network activity.
During a graceful reload, new workers use the new configuration while old workers
finish existing connections.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Master process" eyebrow="Lifecycle" icon="settings" tone="blue" %}
Validate and load configuration, own privileged startup work, and coordinate reload,
reopen, quit, and worker replacement signals.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Worker event loop" eyebrow="Concurrency" icon="activity" tone="cyan" %}
One worker can manage many sockets with non-blocking I/O. Slow network clients do not
require one operating-system thread each.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Shared zones" eyebrow="Cross-worker state" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Rate-limit state, cache metadata, upstream state, and other module data may live in
explicitly sized shared-memory zones.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

`worker_connections` is not a requests-per-second setting. It limits connections a
worker can own, including upstream connections. A proxied request may occupy a client
socket and an upstream socket at the same time, while HTTP/2, WebSockets, keep-alive,
buffering, and slow responses produce different concurrency shapes.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric label="Request concurrency" value="rate x duration" detail="Use latency distributions, not only an average" tone="blue" /%}
{% metric label="Proxy slots" value="downstream + upstream" detail="A proxied miss commonly occupies both sides" tone="cyan" /%}
{% metric label="Configured ceiling" value="workers x worker_connections" detail="Also bound by file descriptors and operating system limits" tone="violet" /%}
{% metric label="Origin pressure" value="misses x retries" detail="Cache and failure policy decide downstream work" tone="rose" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

{% interactive-block id="technology/nginx-performance" dataFile="/api/content/technology/nginx/data/proxy-capacity.json" /%}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/nginx/code/production-reverse-proxy.conf" language="nginx" title="Bound a production reverse-proxy path" /%}

### Benchmark the real resource envelope

- Raise file-descriptor limits only after measuring worker connections, log files,
  cache files, upstream sockets, and reload overlap.
- Measure CPU cost for the deployed TLS versions, ciphers, compression, logging,
  request transformations, and modules.
- Test small and large responses with buffering both enabled and intentionally disabled.
- Include slow clients, long-lived streams, upstream keep-alive, DNS changes, and
  graceful reload in concurrency tests.
- Record p50, p95, and p99 edge and upstream time separately; a healthy average can hide
  an exhausted connection or upstream queue.

See the official [core directive reference](https://nginx.org/en/docs/ngx_core_module.html)
for the exact worker and connection settings supported by the deployed release.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Select a load-balancing method from request behavior

No method knows application capacity automatically. The algorithm observes only the
signals in its contract, while weights, health behavior, connection reuse, session
state, and long requests determine the real distribution.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Round robin" eyebrow="Default" icon="repeat" tone="blue" %}
Rotate requests across available servers. It is a clear baseline when instances and
request costs are similar.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Least connections" eyebrow="Uneven duration" icon="activity" tone="cyan" %}
Send new work to the server with fewer active connections. Connection count is useful
for long or uneven requests but is still only a proxy for load.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Hash routing" eyebrow="Stable affinity" icon="key" tone="violet" %}
Hash a declared key when repeat placement is necessary. Choose a key that reflects the
real affinity boundary and plan behavior when a server disappears.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Weighted capacity" eyebrow="Mixed servers" icon="scale" tone="amber" %}
Give capable servers proportionally more traffic. Maintain weights as instance shape,
limits, and workload change.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Keep health and state outside wishful routing

- Open-source NGINX performs passive checks from live request failures. Know which
  failures count, how `max_fails` and `fail_timeout` interact, and when a server is
  tried again.
- Do not use IP hash as a substitute for external session storage. Carrier NAT,
  proxies, IPv6 behavior, and failover weaken client-to-server affinity.
- Bound upstream keep-alive pools but remember that the `keepalive` directive does not
  cap all connections a worker can open to an upstream group.
- Drain application instances before shutdown and test long requests and upgraded
  connections during rollout.
- Compare per-upstream request count, active work, latency, failures, saturation, and
  business cost rather than declaring a method balanced from totals.

The official [load-balancing guide](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/load_balancing.html)
documents round robin, least connections, hash routing, weights, and passive health
behavior.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Make retries, cache expiry, and client identity fail visibly

A proxy timeout does not prove the application did nothing. An expired hot cache entry
can send every waiting request to the origin. A forwarding header is not trustworthy
merely because its name begins with `X-Forwarded-`.

Use the lab to inject each incident and change the control that protects its actual
boundary. Retry identity, cache-fill ownership, stale policy, and trusted proxy ranges
are independent decisions.

{% interactive-block id="technology/nginx-failure-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/nginx/data/failure-incidents.json" /%}

### Interpret upstream failure precisely

- Set connect, send, and read timeouts from the end-to-end deadline and the upstream's
  measured behavior. A long timeout is not a health strategy.
- Limit retry tries and total retry time. Preserve enough response and log context to
  distinguish connection failure, timeout, invalid response, and explicit status.
- Do not enable retries for non-idempotent requests unless the application uses a
  stable operation ID and returns or converges on the first durable outcome.
- Account for request buffering. The proxy may have sent some or all of a request body
  before an error made the final upstream result unknown.
- Shed load before every worker and upstream queue saturates; overload needs a bounded,
  observable response.

The current [`proxy_next_upstream` reference](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_next_upstream)
defines the failure classes, retry limits, and special treatment of non-idempotent
requests.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Cache only responses with a complete identity and freshness policy

An HTTP cache stores a representation under a cache key. Safety depends on whether the
key includes every request property that changes the response and whether authenticated,
personalized, cookie-setting, or otherwise private responses are excluded.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Classify eligibility" label="Privacy" %}
Permit only methods, statuses, routes, and response metadata with a documented shared-
cache contract. Bypass authorization and session-dependent requests by default.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Build the cache key" label="Identity" %}
Include scheme, host, normalized path/query, and every trusted variation dimension that
changes the representation.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Define freshness" label="Time" %}
Respect application cache headers or an explicit edge policy. Separate freshness from
disk inactivity and eviction.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Control refresh" label="Stampede" %}
Use cache locking, background update, conditional revalidation, and permitted stale
serving to contain origin work.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/nginx/code/cache-with-stale.conf" language="nginx" title="Protect a shared cache from unsafe identity and refresh pressure" /%}

### Test cache correctness before hit rate

- Send two users, authorization states, languages, encodings, devices, and query orders
  through the same route and confirm only equivalent representations share a key.
- Expire one hot object under peak concurrency and count origin requests with and
  without cache locking.
- Fail the origin and verify exactly which statuses may serve stale, for how long, and
  with what user-visible warning or age evidence.
- Purge or version content and confirm every relevant key is removed or made
  unreachable without a global cache flush.
- Monitor hit, miss, bypass, expired, updating, stale, revalidated, origin latency,
  cache-zone capacity, disk capacity, and loader/manager behavior.

The official [proxy-cache directive reference](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_proxy_module.html#proxy_cache)
documents cache keys, shared zones, locking, background update, revalidation, and stale
serving.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Treat forwarding headers and TLS as security boundaries

The direct peer address is trustworthy only to the extent that the network path is
controlled. If NGINX accepts arbitrary client-supplied forwarding headers as identity,
an attacker can influence rate limits, audit logs, geolocation, and access decisions.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Trusted client identity" eyebrow="Known proxy ranges" icon="fingerprint" tone="blue" %}
Strip or replace inbound forwarding fields at the first trusted edge. Configure real-IP
processing only for explicit load-balancer or proxy networks.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Upstream TLS identity" eyebrow="Verify the service" icon="lock" tone="violet" %}
When proxying over HTTPS, enable certificate verification, provide trusted CAs, set the
expected server name, and test rotation and failure.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Bound untrusted input" eyebrow="Admission" icon="shield" tone="amber" %}
Limit header and body size, request rate, connection rate, methods, parse time, and
temporary-file use before expensive upstream work begins.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Keep edge policy auditable

- Prefer allowlists for administrative hosts, paths, methods, and source networks.
- Normalize one request ID at the trusted boundary and reject invalid external values
  before forwarding.
- Avoid logging credentials, authorization values, session cookies, signed URLs, and
  sensitive query parameters.
- Add HSTS and cookie attributes only with an application-aware rollout and rollback
  plan.
- Patch the deployed NGINX distribution and modules, remove unused modules, and verify
  the exact compiled configuration with `nginx -V`.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Reload, observe, and recover as one operational workflow

Graceful reload is powerful because established connections can finish on old workers
while new workers accept traffic with the new configuration. It also means two worker
generations can temporarily consume resources and long-lived connections can keep an
old policy alive.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Render and validate" label="Before change" %}
Generate configuration from reviewed inputs, run syntax validation, and execute route,
header, cache, and retry tests against the candidate.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Reload a small edge set" label="Canary" %}
Apply the candidate to bounded traffic and watch worker startup, upstream selection,
status codes, latency, resource use, and security policy.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Observe generation overlap" label="Drain" %}
Track old workers and long-lived connections. Confirm memory, file descriptors, and
logs remain healthy until the old generation exits.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Promote or roll back" label="Evidence" %}
Expand only when user and upstream outcomes remain inside the release envelope. Restore
the prior artifact when a route or policy regresses.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Monitor consequences, not just process liveness

- Edge request rate, active connections, accepted/handled connections, and worker exits.
- Status codes by route, host, upstream, cache status, retry count, and final attempt.
- Request time, upstream connect time, header time, response time, and bytes buffered.
- Upstream active work, failures, queueing, saturation, and application business errors.
- Cache hit/miss/bypass/stale/update behavior, shared-zone pressure, disk pressure, and
  origin amplification.
- Rejected requests, rate-limit actions, invalid headers, oversized bodies, TLS errors,
  certificate expiry, and forwarding-boundary violations.

The production question is not “How many requests can NGINX handle?” It is “Which
request contract must the edge preserve, and what evidence proves it still does so
during overload, upstream ambiguity, cache expiry, and configuration change?”
{% /section-card %}

